<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Army of Vietnam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PAVN, Vietnam People's Army</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> is the armed force of Vietnam, founded in December 1944 as a small propaganda unit under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vo-nguyen-giap">Vo Nguyen Giap</a>. It fought the French in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954, and defeated South Vietnam and its American allies by 1975. It then invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in 1978 and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-army-of-vietnam.jpg" alt="People's Army of Vietnam" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sshu94, updated by Hwipadam. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_VPA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The People's Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was first conceived in September 1944 as the Information, Communication and Education Unit of the Liberation Army, meant to educate, recruit and mobilise the Vietnamese to drive out the French colonial and Japanese occupiers. Under the guidelines of Hồ Chí Minh, Võ Nguyên Giáp was given the task of establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>, and the Propaganda Unit of the Liberation Army came into existence on 22 December 1944. Its first formation had thirty-one men and three women, armed with two revolvers, seventeen rifles, one light machine gun and fourteen breech-loading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flintlock">flintlocks</a>, and it fought its first engagements at Khai Phat and Na Ngan against French soldiers in late 1944.</p><p>In 1954 Hồ Chí Minh's administration formally named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>'s main armed wing Quân đội nhân dân Việt Nam, the Military of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. In Ho's words the name stressed an army from the People, fighting for the People and serving the People.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the French Indochina War the army was often called the Việt Minh. Its first regiment, the 102nd Capital Regiment, was created on 7 January 1947 for operations around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, and its first division, the 308th, followed. In late 1951 five new divisions were created, and the 351st Division became its first artillery division. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Battle of Dien Bien Phu</a> it received 24 captured US 105 mm howitzers supplied by the Chinese People's Liberation Army. In 1954 four of its divisions, supported by this artillery, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> forces at Dien Bien Phu, ending 83 years of French rule in Indochina.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> its opponents called it the North Vietnamese Army, to tell it from the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a>, though both worked under the same command structure. In 1976, after reunification, the Liberation Army of South Vietnam was disbanded and merged with it. It had forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> to secure the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> trail and support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a>, aided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> in toppling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lon-nol">Lon Nol</a>'s government in 1975, and in 1978 helped topple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a>'s Democratic Kampuchea. It also fought in border wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The People's Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> is the armed wing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a> and the core component of the Vietnam People's Armed Forces. It includes the Ground Force, the Navy, Air Defence and Air Force, the Border Guard and the Coast Guard. It is administered by the Ministry of National Defence under the party's Central Military Commission, which holds most power over the military, although the President of Vietnam is commander-in-chief on paper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscription</a> applies in principle to every male aged 18 to 27.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Army of Vietnam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The invasion of Laos in 1971 was defeated by the <b>PAVN</b>, and the North's conventional invasion of 1972 was repulsed only with massive US air support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>People’s Army of Vietnam</b> emerged from the war against the United States as one of the largest and most experienced armies in the world, and the new state used it almost at once.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first major battle between the United States Army and the <b>People's Army of Vietnam</b> began at the foot of the Chu Pong Massif.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
